diagnosis_text:
After r8 was restarted (evidenced by OSPF adjacency state-changes timestamped ≈3 minutes before investigation began), its ospfd process re-established the OSPF virtual link VLINK0 to r4 (14.1.4.1) through the Area-1 transit area and reached Full adjacency state. However, r8's Area-0 LSDB was left with an incomplete database: it contains 72 LSAs while every other Area-0 router (r4, r3, r1, r9) holds 73. The single missing entry is the **type-4 ASBR-summary LSA** (LS-ID 112.1.1.1, generated by r1/111.1.1.1, metric 10) that advertises the path to ASBR r11 throughout the backbone. Without this LSA in its Area-0 database, r8's OSPF process cannot resolve the next-hop to r11 and therefore installs **zero external (type-5) routes** — none of the 11 EIGRP-redistributed prefixes (102.1.x/24 loopbacks, 103.0.0.0/8, 112.0.0.0/8, 113.1.x/24, 120.0.0.0/8) appear in r8's kernel forwarding table. Any traffic originating from r8's Area-2 stub networks (18.x.x.x, 80.0.0.0/8) attempting to reach EIGRP-domain devices hits an immediate "Network is unreachable" on r8 itself; the virtual link looks up but the routes to cross it toward the external domain simply do not exist. The root cause is that the type-4 ASBR-summary LSA was not properly flooded to r8 through the virtual link during LSDB re-synchronisation after the restart (likely an FRR virtual-link propagation issue triggered by the restart). The corrective fix is to force a fresh OSPF database exchange on r8: run **`clear ip ospf`** in r8's vtysh (or restart ospfd on r8), which tears down and rebuilds all adjacencies and ensures the missing type-4 LSA is received from r4, allowing external routes to be installed.
=== END ===